d "7e554aac-e51a-56ed-97d6-d71fc9b2a0af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065027 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065028 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065028 #edb/ref 1061 13983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065028 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dcc23e94-3ee0-58a9-b2f0-f64588a9d011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065028 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065029 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065029 #edb/ref 1061 13984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065029 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7676fb8a-e4a5-5c86-861b-29726b6f4646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065029 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065034 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065034 #edb/ref 1061 14167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a24545f5-76c6-581e-99d9-e9b0034c2475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065043 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065043 #edb/ref 1061 15296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065043 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9ebe7f3-c1dc-5c5b-9736-209a7e1ed0c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065043 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1064 "What logarithmic equation is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nNotice that the given equation can be written using exponents as\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $a = b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_{b}{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2\n$$\n\nwith $a = b^x$, we get\n\n$$\na = 2, b = 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_{4}{2}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1065 2780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "689eb94a-47df-5cd0-9019-d100d1621193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1067 "Converting Equations With Radicals Into Logarithmic Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1064 "A **logarithm** (or log) is an operation that's the *inverse* of exponentiation.\n\nFor example, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8\n$$\n\nBut we can also write this using a logarithm as\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nIn words, we would say \"log of $8$ with base ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ equals ${\\color{red}{3}}$\". The **base** of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\n\nSince exponents and logarithms are opposite operations, an exponential equation can always be expressed in logarithmic form, and vice versa.\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with positive base\n\n$$\nb \\neq 1\n$$\n\nis defined as follows:\n\n> $a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1065 2863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4581c0d9-ad31-511c-a9d0-00d5ae0d5e38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
